{"name":"Blue Tapestry","hex":"#475c62","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"tapestry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy woven cloth, often with decorative pictorial designs, normally hung on walls.","Anything with variegated or complex details."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To decorate with tapestry, or as if with a tapestry."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tapestry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tapestry","blocks":[{"text":"Tapestry is a form of textile art which was traditionally woven by hand on a loom. Normally it is used to create images rather than patterns. Tapestry is relatively fragile, and difficult to make, so most historical pieces are intended to hang vertically on a wall, or sometimes to lie horizontally over a piece of furniture such as a table or bed. Some periods made smaller pieces, often long and narrow and used as borders for other textiles. Most weavers use a natural warp thread, such as wool, linen, or cotton. The weft threads are usually wool or cotton but may include silk, gold, silver, or other alternatives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Rea-Menzies_in_studio.jpg/330px-Rea-Menzies_in_studio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tapestry","wikidata":"Q184296"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/The_Hunt_of_the_Unicorn_Tapestry_1.jpg/500px-The_Hunt_of_the_Unicorn_Tapestry_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/The_Hunt_of_the_Unicorn_Tapestry_1.jpg/1280px-The_Hunt_of_the_Unicorn_Tapestry_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/The_Hunt_of_the_Unicorn_Tapestry_1.jpg/500px-The_Hunt_of_the_Unicorn_Tapestry_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of the tapestries in the series The Hunt of the Unicorn: The Unicorn is Found, circa 1495–1505, The Cloisters, Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York City","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Hunt_of_the_Unicorn_Tapestry_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Tapestry_Room_from_Croome_Court_MET_DP341243.jpg/500px-Tapestry_Room_from_Croome_Court_MET_DP341243.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Tapestry_Room_from_Croome_Court_